+\paragraph{Power-law comparison.}
+To compare the frequency dependence of gap and novelty, we fit $\log y=\log c-\gamma\log F$ with equal weight on the same 18 bins, representing each bin by the geometric mean of its endpoints. At step 1000 all three-seed mean gaps are positive, so no bins are removed for the log fit. Table~\ref{tab:coverage-exponents} reports both branches. The bigram exponents are close, $0.251$ versus $0.229$, whereas the trigram gap decays more steeply and departs more from a power law. These are finite-range descriptive fits, not universal corpus exponents; the conditional-tail argument in Equation~\eqref{eq:occupancy} does not by itself fix the exponent of a cross-context fit.
